i must give the british people a very  simple  instruction you must stay at home  he said he hopes never to issue that  instruction again  but in order to avoid another full  national lockdown  new national restrictions are once again  being imposed  in response to rising cases the latest  step  making self-isolation a legal  requirement in england  enforceable with fines of up to ten  thousand pounds  in a statement the prime minister said  the best way we can fight this virus is  by everyone following the rules and  self-isolating if they're at risk of  passing on coronavirus  and so nobody underestimates just how  important this  is new regulations will mean you are  legally obliged to do so  if you have the virus or have been asked  to do so by nhs test and trace  people who choose to ignore the rules  will face significant fines  to assist those facing financial  pressures the government has also  announced a self-isolation payment of  500 pounds  for those on low incomes but some in the  scientific community  have warned it's not just an issue of  compliance they say more effective  testing and tracing  should be a priority the question is you  know are we going to keep doing this and  keep seeing these peaks  until such point as we have a roll out  of a nationwide vaccine  i hope not but the only way we can do  that is through making sure we have the  mechanisms and tools in place  to be able to stop that and that is a  functioning testing system  and a functioning contact racing system  the government have made clear the  outright closure of schools and  businesses would be a last resort  but consideration is being given to the  national rollout  of measures already in place in local  hotspots these include bans on  socializing outside of your own  household  and restrictions on pubs and restaurants  that would see them close at 10 pm  labour say the government need to show  some urgency in coming to a decision  at the start of this pandemic earlier in  the year the government were  too slow they were slow to lock down  they were slow on personal protective  equipment  slow on testing and i'm afraid they  still haven't got  test track and isolate anywhere near the  level that it should be  so what we're saying to the government  is don't be  slow again new local restrictions for  parts of the northeast have just taken  effect in blyth  some here who switched their vote from  labour to the conservatives at the last  election  are now regretting that decision thought  they were brilliant mushy soon i thought  they were brilliant but then  as time's gone on we don't know we don't  think they know what they do i don't  like the man now but there you go  but others were more forgiving i think  he has a very difficult task  very difficult and i don't think anyone  else in his position could have done any  better  back in westminster it's not clear all  conservative mps will show the same  patience  it's less than a week since the rule of  six was introduced across the country  and the prime minister is already  considering whether he needs to go  further but as part of that calculation  he'll be aware that there are those in  his party who believe the  current restrictions already go too far  and are planning a potential  parliamentary challenge to the existing  rules  in the coming days for a prime minister  keen to show he's guided by the science  that will further complicate his options  doing too little  or too much could both come at a  considerable cost  tom rayner sky news westminster
 

